    "notes": "HALF NOISE, sigma 0.025. Declared with a quantitative prediction BEFORE running and never executed until now. Tests whether the stability/sensitivity trade-off has a usable middle. Measured endpoints, body_vel corr at 0.1 s, n=200 val: pre-noise (sigma 0) 0.668 [0.583,0.738] PASSES the gate; full-noise (sigma 0.05) 0.495 [0.382,0.593] INDETERMINATE. Contraction buys rollout stability and costs action sensitivity. PREDICTION: corr at 0.1 s lands between 0.495 and 0.668 if contraction scales with noise magnitude. THE USEFUL OUTCOME is different and harder: a surrogate that is BOTH stable to ~0.5 s AND passes corr THERE, which would let branches be longer than 0.1 s on a certified model and make the short-branch structural hypothesis testable. If no such middle exists the trade-off is intrinsic and we stop."
